docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:124:- [x] normalize relation inference for explicit and inferred `before`, `after`, `during`, `overlaps`, and `same_time` — `build_temporal_relation_registry` now marks explicit relations as `inference_mode="explicit"` and generates inferred `before`/`same_time` relations from date anchors with `inference_mode="derived_from_date_anchors"` for pairs not already covered by an explicit relation
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:125:- [x] formalize issue categories such as `missing_anchor`, `contradictory_dates`, `relative_only_ordering`, and `limitations_risk` — `build_temporal_issue_registry` now normalises `temporal_contradictory_dates` → `contradictory_dates`, `temporal_limitations_risk` → `limitations_risk`, etc. from the contradiction queue so rule profiles and downstream consumers see canonical category names
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:127:- [x] emit deterministic claim-level temporal graph summaries for packets and review payloads — `build_claim_temporal_graphs()` now assembles `claim_temporal_graphs` from canonical fact, relation, and issue registries, and `_get_temporal_reasoning_context()` consumes that graph when mediator status provides it
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:128:- [x] preserve relation previews and type counts from the graph, not from UI formatting logic — `relation_type_counts`, `relation_preview`, `warnings`, and readiness trace IDs are carried through graph `consistency_summary` → `temporal_summary` → review payload
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:129:- [x] trace temporal readiness to graph IDs — `intake_chronology_readiness` now includes `trace_fact_ids`, `trace_relation_ids`, `trace_issue_ids`, and `claim_temporal_graph_ids` derived from `claim_temporal_graphs`
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:133:- claim-level temporal summaries are reproducible from one graph assembly path
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:134:- packet-level temporal readiness is traceable to underlying fact and relation IDs
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:135:- temporal warnings align with actual graph issues, not loose heuristics
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:144:- `.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_t1_t3_temporal_next_steps.py -q`
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:145:- `.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_claim_support_hooks.py tests/test_mediator_three_phase.py -q`
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:165:- [x] define a temporal rule profile contract with required events, optional events, deadlines, and defenses — `TemporalRuleProfileContract`, `TemporalEventRequirement`, and `LegalTemporalWindow` in `complaint_analysis/temporal_rule_profiles.py` expose data-only contracts through `list_temporal_rule_profiles()`, `get_temporal_rule_profile_contract()`, and `get_temporal_rule_profile_for_claim_type()`
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:168:- [x] expose rule-frame IDs in proof payloads so failures can be explained against concrete legal rules — `rule_frame_id` in proof bundles and `temporal_rule_frame_id` in element review items
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:169:- [x] document how claim-type timing rules differ from generic timeline consistency warnings — see `_TEMPORAL_ISSUE_FOLLOW_UP_PROFILES` and the profile registry in `complaint_analysis/temporal_rule_profiles.py`; claim-type rules (e.g. `retaliation_temporal_profile_v1`) evaluate ordered role-tagged facts against a legal frame (`retaliation_temporal_frame`) whereas generic timeline warnings come from issue-registry normalization of `missing_anchor`, `contradictory_dates`, and `relative_only_ordering` issue types without a claim-type gate
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:170:- [x] expose non-rendering discovery hooks — `get_temporal_rule_question_hints()` in `complaint_analysis/decision_trees.py` and `get_temporal_legal_patterns()` in `complaint_analysis/legal_patterns.py` let intake/review code discover the same profile events and legal timing terms without depending on HTML templates
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:180:- if a claim type lacks a rule profile, the system falls back to generic temporal consistency rather than pretending legal sufficiency exists
